#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "3d5ffe46-c0ac-5309-a31b-28a6a1d927e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9054" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n2. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n3. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 \\neq \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nRecall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$.*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "30daf182-0ed6-5ddd-994e-a5af7c6c6c01" :question/math-academy-id "e-9056" :question/problem "$u→^(A + 3 I)v→^(A + 3 I)w→^(A + 3 I)0; x→^(A - 5 I)y→^(A - 5 I)0$\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+3I$ and $A-5I$ on the nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w},\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n2. $\\mathbf{u}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n3. $\\mathbf{y}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v}_n$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n$\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0}$,\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nAn *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n$\\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A+3I} \\mathbf{w} \\xrightarrow{A+3I} \\mathbf{0}$,\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}2$ steps,\nthe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank ${\\color{red}2}$, not $1$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n$\\mathbf{u} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{w} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{0}$,\ni.e. $A+{\\color{blue}3}I$ maps $\\mathbf{u}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}3$ steps,\nthe vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $\\color{red}3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda={\\color{blue}-3}$ of $A$.\n- Statement III is false. Since\n$\\mathbf{y} \\xrightarrow{A-{\\color{black}5}I} \\mathbf{0}$,\ni.e. $A-{\\color{black}5}I$ maps $\\mathbf{y}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}1$ step,\nthe vector $\\mathbf{y}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $\\color{red}1$, not $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151963 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "79ffef9f-8be0-581f-b2f1-dc796d2cf94b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9057" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n2. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n3. $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n$\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0}$,\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nAn *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$.\n\n- First, we compute $A-I{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$.*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a209982b-cb18-55e5-b09e-0d2d4f332fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148765} {:answer/id #uuid "fffc6e57-dec5-5b32-8e66-3233670c8659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148766} {:answer/id #uuid "169ecf63-4076-50c6-94cd-76cffb554498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148767} {:answer/id #uuid "266b3eba-6045-5104-9608-589d5bd1cf50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148768} {:answer/id #uuid "50041473-2324-553a-9a74-b20330263d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "169ecf63-4076-50c6-94cd-76cffb554498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148767} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e9e410a-3054-5995-8499-24512b0280d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148764}] :question/id #uuid "86e7e59c-5f60-5647-9c15-90e1d3a1ac43" :question/math-academy-id "q-109113" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 3$ of $A$\n2. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 3$ of $A$\n3. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 3$ of $A$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eee89eb7-deb5-5561-bcef-87fe270ce52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148783} {:answer/id #uuid "2501b1c2-807f-51b4-a2fd-eb1376cfd7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148784} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8df64b-4747-5783-90f4-8b0602f55568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148785} {:answer/id #uuid "554903b1-3664-5acc-ac17-b3ca5dc73f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148786} {:answer/id #uuid "6bae7c05-3161-59b5-97fb-9a3d9e245824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "554903b1-3664-5acc-ac17-b3ca5dc73f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148786} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d7d4d84-f91a-5129-b785-67b83ec86582" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148782}] :question/id #uuid "13d405ca-1969-5fbd-88a4-d12478e301e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109500" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$ of $A$\n2. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$ of $A$\n3. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$ of $A$"}]}
